Ankle Sprain
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Mechanism of inversion/eversion, Localized perimalleolar swelling, Positive anterior drawer/talar tilt

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-005
Reason for Visit: Acute traumatic ankle injury with rapid swelling and complete inability to bear weight.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage established a high-velocity inversion injury mechanism accompanied by an audible pop and subsequent inability to weight-bear. Urgent clinical evaluation applying the Ottawa Ankle Rules was mandated to rule out an acute displaced fracture, prevent inappropriate mobilization that could worsen tissue disruption, and initiate early acute trauma protocols prior to standard clinical operating hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I rolled my ankle stepping off the curb, heard a loud pop, and now it's swollen like a balloon and I can't stand on it."
History of Present Illness: Acute onset of pain and swelling immediately following an inversion mechanism of injury 3 hours ago. Timeline: Injury occurred stepping off a curb with immediate sharp pain; audible tearing sensation reported at the moment of trauma; progressive, rapid localized swelling along the lateral aspect of the ankle; patient managed to hop indoors but has been entirely non-weight-bearing since.
Functional Status: Acutely immobilized, utilizing hopping for short indoor distances.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to single-leg hopping without appropriate mobility aids.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, in mild distress, applying a bag of frozen peas to the elevated ankle.
Physical Exam: Joint effusion leading to loss of the normal lateral perimalleolar anatomical contours. Localized ecchymosis beginning to appear inferior to the lateral malleolus. Exquisite point tenderness located specifically over the anterior talofibular ligament. Palpation of the posterior edge and tip of the lateral and medial malleoli, base of the 5th metatarsal, and navicular bone reveals no distinct bony tenderness. Mildly increased anterior translation of the talus on anterior drawer test, though limited by guarding.
Environmental Observations: Walkways are clear, but patient requires a mobility aid to safely navigate to the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken yet.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Lateral Ankle Sprain (Grade II) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Lateral Malleolus Fracture - Ruled out clinically by strict application of the Ottawa Ankle Rules (no bony tenderness at the posterior edge/tip of the malleolus).

Fifth Metatarsal Avulsion Fracture - Ruled out due to absence of focal bony tenderness at the base of the fifth metatarsal.

Achilles Tendon Rupture - Ruled out due to intact Thompson squeeze test and mechanism of injury being isolated inversion rather than forced dorsiflexion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained an acute inversion injury to the ankle resulting in significant pain, structural swelling, and functional non-weight-bearing status. Postponing evaluation risked exposing the patient to unmanaged pain, improper initial mobilization, and the potential exacerbation of a misdiagnosed fracture. Urgent after-hours assessment permitted the rigorous application of the Ottawa Ankle Rules, which clinically excluded a major fracture and bypassed the immediate need for emergency department radiography. This allowed for the prompt initiation of definitive conservative management, provision of mobility aids to mitigate fall risk, and effective pharmacological analgesia in the home setting.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for 5 days. Applied a firm crepe compression bandage.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Elevate the ankle above the level of the heart while resting. Consequence: Gravity assists fluid drainage, leading to visibly reduced throbbing and swelling within hours (negative reinforcement).
Action: Perform slow, pain-free ankle circles (drawing the alphabet) while elevated. Consequence: Maintenance of joint mobility, making the first steps in the morning significantly less stiff and painful (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral provided for functional rehabilitation.
DME/Safety (Optional): Supplied standard axillary crutches and provided instruction on a partial weight-bearing gait.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days for re-examination once acute swelling subsides.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the foot becomes cold, pale, or blue, if there is uncontrolled severe pain despite medication, or if numbness develops in the toes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical and excludes obvious fractures based on validated guidelines; a minor occult fracture remains possible. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Swelling and bruising will peak over the next 48 hours. Ligament healing takes 4-6 weeks, requiring dedicated rehabilitation to restore proprioception and prevent chronic instability.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if your toes turn pale or blue, if you lose sensation in your foot, or if the pain becomes completely unmanageable.

PATIENT EDUCATION

